WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.901
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy Award-winning actor

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:05.133
and comedian who co-created
and stars in the critically

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:06.534
acclaimed series "Ted Lasso."

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:08.667
New episodes of the third season

00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:10.801
are available Wednesdays on
Apple TV+.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:11.868
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:13.801
our very good friend
Jason Sudeikis.

00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:15.701
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:22.968
♪♪

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:30.234
♪♪

00:00:30.234 --> 00:00:33.033
Welcome back.
It is so nice to have you.

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:34.334
-Nice to be had.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.000
-It's nice to have you back in
New York City.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.501
-Tell me about it, man.
This place --

00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:38.634
I don't know how it runs
without me.

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.334
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:41.767
It's like me and the subway.

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:44.067
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:46.234
-You were saying backstage you
were so happy 'cause

00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:48.434
you've clocked that don't have
to get dressed up to come on

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:50.067
this show anymore because I'm
keeping it casual.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.667
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Not that -- It doesn't matter.

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:53.400
I wouldn't have gotten
dressed up anyway.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.634
But this just looks better.
-You did say that

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:57.701
and then I remembered a bunch of
times where I was in a suit

00:00:57.701 --> 00:00:58.868
and you were dressed
just like that.

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:00.467
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:03.133
I don't want to stick out
when I'm riding on the subway.

00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:06.033
I want people to be like,
"There's no way that's him."

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:07.601
-The subway's getting a bad rap
right now.

00:01:07.601 --> 00:01:10.000
-Why? What happened?
-Well, it's not --

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.033
-Jimmy Johns, everybody's on
Jimmy Johns now, right?

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.834
-Oh, you're talking about
the sandwich shop.

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:16.834
-The best New York sandwich...

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:18.133
[ Laughter ]
-I see.

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:21.367
-...is at this little deli.
-I see what happened.

00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:24.367
You've never had a meatball sub
until you've gone to Subway.

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:26.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.567
They keep 'em little.

00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:29.200
You know, a lot of like --
classic New York is

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.667
big old meatballs but the
little, real little ones.

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:34.434
-They're so authentic, you can
bring your passport into

00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:37.400
a Subway and they will stamp it
saying you've been to Italy.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.968
You've been to Italy if you take
a bite of

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:42.767
this Subway meatball sandwich.

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:44.601
No, what's going on
on the subway? How can I help?

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:46.434
-Well, anything you can do.

00:01:46.434 --> 00:01:48.334
Right now, just even talking
about it like this

00:01:48.334 --> 00:01:49.934
so enthusiastically
is gonna be good.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:51.868
This is gonna increase
ridership.

00:01:51.868 --> 00:01:54.067
You know, it's just -- it's not
as safe I think as it was

00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:55.734
when you were kind of
full time here.

00:01:55.734 --> 00:01:57.434
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
That's not good.

00:01:57.434 --> 00:01:58.834
-No, it's not good.

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:01.968
-It's back to being like the
Michael Jackson "Bad" video.

00:02:01.968 --> 00:02:04.601
-Yeah.
-It's taken a downturn.

00:02:04.601 --> 00:02:06.868
-It's like that.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:10.200
-All that stuff.
-But not as choreographed.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.734
-Well, that's where you're gonna
feel danger.

00:02:12.734 --> 00:02:14.033
-It's Michael Jackson "Bad"
video

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:15.200
without the dancing
or the music.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.300
Which some would say are the two
best parts of that video.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.868
-Yes, some would argue that.
-Some would argue that.

00:02:21.868 --> 00:02:25.033
-Well, okay. I'm still going to
ride it every now and then,

00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:27.267
but I'll keep an eye on it.
-By the way, I still find it

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.601
a very efficient way
to get around.

00:02:28.601 --> 00:02:30.834
-Yeah, absolutely. It is.
It's great.

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:33.234
-I want to ask of your kids.

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:35.801
Obviously they've gotten to
spend some time in England

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:37.701
thanks to the show.
They're back and forth.

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:40.300
How are they -- Do they care
about things like soccer?

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.834
Have they fallen in love
with it?

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:44.434
-Oddly enough,
Otis has fallen more

00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:46.267
in love with it
since being back here.

00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:49.467
We got back in like November
and we're out in L.A. right now.

00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:52.133
But he was there almost -- you
know, him and Daisy were out

00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:55.434
there for almost all of 2022.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:57.567
And they started to get little
tiny accents but not enough.

00:02:57.567 --> 00:03:01.767
They got as far as like --
I don't know --

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:03.167
Dick Van Dyke in "Mary Poppins."

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:06.033
[ Laughter ]
It's like that.

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:08.601
-So not enough for people to
know they just spent a year

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:10.267
in England to be like,
"What happened?

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:11.434
What's going on here?"

00:03:11.434 --> 00:03:13.801
-"Oh, someone's laying in
on their Disney+ a lot."

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:16.067
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:18.300
Just only watch, "Hello."

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.534
It's like that.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.534 --> 00:03:21.400
-Yeah, that's jarring.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.033
-But yeah, he's really into
soccer right now.

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:29.467
And he did this thing where he
loves looking up their cards

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:31.400
and, like, videos, and he comes
up with fun facts

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.868
that he finds on YouTube.

00:03:32.868 --> 00:03:34.834
Like, if I bring up a player or
something like that.

00:03:34.834 --> 00:03:35.968
I don't have any off the top of
my head.

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:38.000
But like Messi. He's great.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.667
He loves Messi and Mbappe
and all those guys

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:42.334
from watching the World Cup,
and he's like, "Yeah, but,

00:03:42.334 --> 00:03:45.133
you know, Messi, he hasn't
always been perfect."

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:47.634
I was like --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:47.634 --> 00:03:51.067
Then I look in his history, on
like his YouTube, and it's like

00:03:51.067 --> 00:03:53.133
12 facts you'd never believe
about Lio Messi.

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:54.334
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:56.100
I'm like I'm glad
he's watching that

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.400
instead of TMZ or something.
-That's true.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.834
Poor Messi. A 9-year-old's
walking around being like,

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:02.367
"Hey, don't put him
on a pedestal."

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:04.234
-I know, I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.234 --> 00:04:06.367
It's like, "Son, glass houses.

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:09.868
Don't throw stones."
There's a teachable moment here.

00:04:09.868 --> 00:04:13.968
-This is very sweet to hear that
your son and daughter do --

00:04:13.968 --> 00:04:15.601
they do some music together.

00:04:15.601 --> 00:04:17.100
-They love making up songs.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.167
I come home from work.

00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:19.534
And work right now is editing.

00:04:19.534 --> 00:04:23.234
So I'm sitting in there with
my friends, Mel and AJ,

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:25.434
and we're getting after it
trying to make this TV show.

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:27.367
Then I come home, and as I'm
coming up the stairs,

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:29.501
I can usually hear a keyboard.

00:04:29.501 --> 00:04:33.033
I bought two karaoke setups
for me and Forte.

00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:34.701
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:34.701 --> 00:04:36.334
-But they've now figured out
how to turn them on.

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:37.601
-Uh-huh.

00:04:37.601 --> 00:04:38.934
-And they'll just be
making up songs.

00:04:38.934 --> 00:04:42.067
And I truly believe that it was
because I had the --

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:43.501
What is it called when you think
of something?

00:04:43.501 --> 00:04:45.267
-Like the wherewithal?
-The wherewithal, yeah.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:48.000
To watch the Billie Eilish
documentary.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.501
And so when you see Billie
and her older brother Finneas

00:04:51.501 --> 00:04:55.434
making music together and making
each other laugh,

00:04:55.434 --> 00:04:57.434
it was kind of similar to, like,
when I went to go --

00:04:57.434 --> 00:04:59.400
when I would go visit,
like my Uncle George

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.968
on the "Cheers" set and be like,
"This is a job?

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.801
You guys all joking around
and, like,

00:05:03.801 --> 00:05:05.567
you know, screwing around?"
And it's like --

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:09.133
I think they took that and like,
"Okay, we're gonna do this."

00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:12.067
-Also, a very good sibling
relationship for your kids.

00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:14.534
-100%, yeah.
-This is incredibly supportive

00:05:14.534 --> 00:05:17.300
and laughter --
that's very, very well played.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.834
-Unintentional.
I was just curious because

00:05:19.834 --> 00:05:21.234
Dais and I were gonna to go
to the concert

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:23.033
and I wanted her to have
a little more context because,

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:26.667
you know, you gotta understand
why she's a bad guy.

00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:29.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.834
-You don't wanna go --
Otherwise you're missing it.

00:05:30.834 --> 00:05:32.534
-Yeah.
Oh, yeah, the beat's great,

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:34.701
the lyrics are interesting,
but why is she a bad guy?

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:36.667
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:39.267
-Obviously, you and I -- for my
money, I always say I feel like

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:42.400
we were part of a really special
cast in our time at "SNL."

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.534
-Top 20.
-Top 20.

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:47.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:47.868 --> 00:05:51.133
But I'm so impressed --
-I don't mind talking [bleep]

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:52.567
for our generation because I --

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:54.367
like, I felt like I had such
a good seat there.

00:05:54.367 --> 00:05:58.133
And I can name anybody and not
name my name for like 10 names

00:05:58.133 --> 00:06:00.167
and you're still like, "What?
All at the same time?"

00:06:00.167 --> 00:06:03.200
-Yeah. The fun thing now is
we've been gone long enough,

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.601
people forget who exactly was
all there at the same time.

00:06:05.601 --> 00:06:07.501
-Yeah, yeah, it's remarkable.
-It's a really good time.

00:06:07.501 --> 00:06:10.067
It makes me very happy. But I'm
also -- I don't know, man.

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:11.834
I feel like you did this
really special thing here,

00:06:11.834 --> 00:06:13.601
which is you put together
this group of people.

00:06:13.601 --> 00:06:16.467
And it is just so much fun
to watch them all and --

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:18.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:18.234 --> 00:06:20.467
-It really is.
-You guys, as writers,

00:06:20.467 --> 00:06:22.400
you've taken such good care of
everybody.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.033
And you've given them so many
moves and it is so cool.

00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:26.534
You know, I just started
watching season three.

00:06:26.534 --> 00:06:29.033
But you continue -- you know, it
just feels like people

00:06:29.033 --> 00:06:30.734
get introduced and you take care
of them.

00:06:30.734 --> 00:06:33.667
And it's really -- it just must
be such a cool thing.

00:06:33.667 --> 00:06:35.400
-It really is.
And it is something that --

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:38.334
and I was was saying this to
Eric in wardrobe who, you know,

00:06:38.334 --> 00:06:39.834
we worked with at "SNL."

00:06:39.834 --> 00:06:42.467
Like, it's the lessons you sort
of learn there by either hook

00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:45.534
or crook or sink or swim that
you then, almost in

00:06:45.534 --> 00:06:46.934
a "Karate Kid" type way,
it's like,

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:49.467
"I'm just painting a fence."
It's like, "No, no, no.

00:06:49.467 --> 00:06:52.234
You can cast, you can write,
you can edit."

00:06:52.234 --> 00:06:54.667
And you learn these things from
doing it, doing, it doing it.

00:06:54.667 --> 00:06:58.634
But the biggest thing was
learning to trust in people's,

00:06:58.634 --> 00:07:01.200
you know, taste and for them
to take care of themselves

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.767
and encourage them to take care
of themselves.

00:07:02.767 --> 00:07:04.167
So many of those things.
I mean, Brett's such

00:07:04.167 --> 00:07:05.467
a great example of that story

00:07:05.467 --> 00:07:07.400
'cause he was a writer
on the show.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:08.534
Brett Goldstein.
Then he was like,

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:10.033
"I think I understand
this character.

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:12.267
Can I audition for it?"
Makes a tape and crushes it.

00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:13.634
And he had the gumption
to do that

00:07:13.634 --> 00:07:14.934
and then the talent
to back it up.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.267
And it's kinda like --
that's great.

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:17.968
So you want everyone in every
department to do that.

00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:19.501
So we try to do that
on the writing side.

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:21.567
And here, I mean, as great as
this group is, we're missing

00:07:21.567 --> 00:07:23.300
Juno Temple and Nick Mohammed
in there.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.601
-It's nuts to look at.
It's a very deep roster.

00:07:26.601 --> 00:07:28.334
-It is. I look at that, I'm kind
of like, this is --

00:07:28.334 --> 00:07:30.000
this is like that the poster of
"The Simpsons" when it

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.634
has every character
from Springfield.

00:07:31.634 --> 00:07:33.834
It's like no wonder the shows
are so damn long.

00:07:33.834 --> 00:07:35.267
It's like yeah, we're trying to,
like --

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:36.434
we introduce all these folks.

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:38.300
We're trying to show how
they became what they are

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.267
or just give a glimpse to it.

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:44.133
Then it turns the show into a
bunch of inside jokes in a way.

00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:46.801
-Well, it's a nice feeling
because the nice thing you guys

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:48.400
have done is built a world
where your audience is

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:49.734
on the inside with you guys.
-Yeah.

00:07:49.734 --> 00:07:51.033
-I got a bunch more questions.

00:07:51.033 --> 00:07:52.634
We'll be right back with more
from Jason Sudeikis.

